UID:c4d08de87d3d7a8e@role-business-corporate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261013T224313Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261013
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Labor Department finds 6 New York\, New Je
 rsey construction employers exposed workers to safety hazards\, assesses c
 ombined $648K in penalties
DESCRIPTION:MONROE\, NY – The U.S. Department of Labor has cited six con
 struction employers for willful and serious safety violations involving ex
 cavation hazards at the site of a planned 408\,000 square-foot mixed-use c
 ommercial building and proposed a combined $648\,094 in penalties. The de
 partment’s Occupational Safety and Health Administration responded to an
  allegation of struck-by and collapse hazards associated with a large exca
 vation at the Monroe Commons project – also known as Magna Square develo
 pment – a planned 4-story\, approximately 408\,000 square-foot mixed-use
  commercial building with retail\, office space\, and a 39-room hotel loca
 ted on an 18-acre site. The excavation had a steep\, unstable embankment n
 early 75 feet high which was not adequately sloped or shored. OSHA issued
  a total of 23 serious and five willful citations to the project’s gener
 al contractor and five subcontractors for failing to protect employees fro
 m cave-in\, struck-by\, and chemical hazards\; neglecting to shield employ
 ees from falling or rolling rock and soil from an excavation face\; and no
 t ensuring daily inspections of excavations were conducted by a competent 
 person. Advanced Plan and Build LLC\, and subcontractors Parkstone Inc. a
 nd Obstacle Systems Inc. – operating as Drip Drop Waterproofing Inc. –
  and The Paving Masters LLC received citations for willfully exposing empl
 oyees to cave-in and struck-by hazards from an unprotected excavation. OSH
 A also cited Advanced Plan and Build and The Paving Masters for their will
 ful failure to ensure that the excavation\, which was between 21 and 75 fe
 et deep\, was designed by a registered professional engineer. Subcontract
 ors Century Max Industries Corp.\, DelValle Contractor\, and Prime Structu
 re Inc. were cited for serious violations for failing to protect employees
  from cave-in and struck-by hazards. DelValle and Parkstone Inc. and Obsta
 cle Systems Inc. also received citations for serious violations related to
  lack of fall protection and chemical hazards.OSH\nhttp://www.dol.gov/news
 room/releases/osha/osha20261013
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20261013
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:13f3e985b9c3022e@role-business-corporate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261013T224217Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261013
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] The MaineCare Third Party Liability (TPL) Exe
 mption Request and Attestation Form is Now Available
DESCRIPTION:Maine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Ma
 ine.gov\nDHHS\n→\nOffice of MaineCare Services (OMS)\n→\nProvider Reso
 urces\n→\n	 \n			The MaineCare Third Party Liability (TPL) Exemption Req
 uest and Attestation Form is Now Available\nThe MaineCare Third Party Liab
 ility (TPL) Exemption Request and Attestation Form is Now Available\nOct 1
 3\, 2026\nOn\nAugust 3\, 2026\n\, MaineCare announced claims system update
 s to support proper TPL reimbursement processes in accordance with MaineCa
 re Benefits Manual (MBM)\, Chapter I\, Section 1.07 TPL rules. The\nMaineC
 are TPL Exemption Request and Attestation Form\nis now available for TPL e
 xemption request submission.\nRequesting TPL Exemption\nIn accordance with
  MBM\, Chapter I\, Section 1.07 rules\, MaineCare may assess the validity 
 of extenuating circumstance or service code TPL exemption requests to bill
  MaineCare as the primary payer for services delivered to a member with pr
 imary\, public\, or commercial coverage. Providers wishing to apply for TP
 L exemption to bill MaineCare as the primary payer must complete and submi
 t the\nMaineCare TPL Exemption Request and Attestation Form\nto the\nTPL M
 edicare Code Exempt Request email box\n.\nMaineCare will review completed 
 forms with the supporting documentation and provide a decision letter of a
 pproval or non-approval via email to the requestor. An exemption request a
 pproval letter is valid only for a specific member for a defined duration\
 , no greater than 12 months. The exemption request approval letter must be
  attached to all applicable MaineCare claims for dates of service during t
 he approved time. Claims without a valid and up-to-date approval letter ma
 y result in denials.\nProviders are responsible for maintaining exemption 
 request renewals by submitting a subsequent MaineCare\nTPL Exemption Reque
 st and Attestation Form\nat least 30 calendar days prior to the expiration
  date specified in the approval letter.\nLimited Retroactive TPL Exemption
  Review Period\nFor a limited time\, MaineCare will consider conti\nhttps:
 //www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/mainecare-third-part
 y-liability-tpl-exemption-request-and-attestation-form-now-available
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/mainecare-
 third-party-liability-tpl-exemption-request-and-attestation-form-now-avail
 able
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e211f8de2c3ba8b8@role-business-corporate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261013T224142Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261013
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine Day of Soccer - October 15
DESCRIPTION:WHEREAS\n\, since their founding\, the Hearts of Pine have bro
 ught together soccer fans new and old from across Maine to join in a love 
 for the game\, community and our state\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, the Hearts have 
 been a remarkable success on and off the field\, reaching the semifinals o
 f the playoffs in their inaugural season\, selling out over 30 consecutive
  games at Fitzpatrick Stadium\, and attracting fans from in and out of Mai
 ne to Portland for the experience\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, the USL W League is a
  pre-professional women’s soccer league that brings high level college-e
 ligible athletes together for a summer season running from May-July\; and\
 nWHEREAS\n\, women in sports deserve greater visibility and opportunity\, 
 and Maine people are ready for a women’s team that reflects the talent\,
  ambition\, and spirit that already exists across this state\; and\nWHEREA
 S\n\, in town halls\, community gatherings\, virtual discussions\, and one
 -on-one conversations\, Maine people have demonstrated their excitement fo
 r bringing a women’s team to Portland\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, 5\,000 season t
 ickets were sold out in just one hour\, further representing the energy al
 ready behind the team\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, we eagerly await the start of the
  2027 season to welcome our new USL W team home\;\nNOW THEREFORE\n\, be it
  resolved that I\, Janet T. Mills\, Governor of the State of Maine\, do he
 reby proclaim October 15\, 2026 as\nMaine Day of Soccer\nthroughout the st
 ate\, and I urge all residents to give a warm welcome to our new USL W fra
 nchise in Maine.\nIn testimony whereof\, I have caused\nthe Great Seal of 
 the State to be\nhereunto affixed GIVEN under my\nhand at Augusta this eig
 hth day of\nOctober Two Thousand Twenty-Six\nhttp://www.maine.gov/governor
 /mills/official_documents/proclamations/2026-10-maine-day-soccer-october-1
 5
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/official_documents/proclamations/2
 026-10-maine-day-soccer-october-15
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
